@* One contributor, as a card in their tier's colour.

   The word count is the only input. There is no accuracy score and no ranking against other
   people — see the note at the top of Services/Tier.cs for why that boundary matters and where
   it came from. A card says "you have written 418 words, that is زێڕ ٢, 82 more reaches زێڕ ١":
   a target of your own, not a position in a race.

   Colour comes from the TIER, never from the person. That distinction is what keeps this from
   being the per-user colour coding the roster deliberately avoids — two people on زێڕ ٢ look
   identical, and a card changes colour only when its owner earns it. *@

<div class="contrib-card @($"contrib-{Level.Key}")">

    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
        @* Ringed in their metal. With a picture the ring is the only tier mark on the avatar;
           without one the fallback circle carries the metal itself, so the tier reads either way. *@
        <Avatar Url="@Contributor.AvatarUrl"
                Name="@(Contributor.FullName ?? Contributor.UserName)"
                Size="40"
                Ring="@($"tier-{Level.Key}-grad")"
                FallbackBackground="@($"tier-{Level.Key}-grad text-white")" />

        <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
            <p class="truncate text-sm font-medium text-ink-1">
                @(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Contributor.FullName) ? Contributor.UserName : Contributor.FullName)
            </p>
            @* Amethyst, from the client palette sheet: this counts a PERSON'S work. The green
               figures on this dashboard all mean "done", and a contributor's total is not a
               completion — it is a running tally that never finishes.

               BOTH halves, always. Written and fixed are different jobs and most people do more
               of one than the other: authorship alone misses the whole team's station work, since
               the imported words have no author, and fixes alone miss whoever typed new headwords.
               The big number is the sum; the two under it are what it is made of, so nobody has to
               guess which kind of work it is counting. Zeros are printed rather than hidden — «٠
               ڕێکخراو» is a true and useful thing to know about a new colleague. *@
            <p class="mt-0.5 flex items-baseline gap-1">
                <bdi class="num-users text-20 font-medium">@Ku.N(Contributor.TotalContribution)</bdi>
                <span class="text-11 text-ink-3">کاری کراو</span>
            </p>

            <p class="mt-0.5 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 text-11 text-ink-3">
                <span><bdi class="text-ink-2">@Ku.N(Contributor.WordCount)</bdi> نووسراو</span>
                <span>·</span>
                <span><bdi class="text-ink-2">@Ku.N(Contributor.WordsUpdated)</bdi> ڕێکخراو</span>
            </p>
        </div>

        <span class="tier @($"tier-{Level.Key}") shrink-0 self-start">
            <Icon Name="medal" Class="h-3.5 w-3.5" />
            <bdi>@Level.Label</bdi>
        </span>
    </div>

    @if (ShowProgress)
    {
        <div class="mt-3">
            <div class="tier-track">
                <div class="tier-fill @($"tier-{Level.Key}-grad")" style="width:@(Pct)%"></div>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-1.5 truncate text-11 text-ink-3">
                @if (Tier.Next(Contributor.TotalContribution) is { } next)
                {
                    <bdi>@Ku.N(Tier.ToNext(Contributor.TotalContribution))</bdi>
                    <text> وشەی تر بۆ </text>
                    <bdi>@next.Label</bdi>
                }
                else
                {
                    <text>بەرزترین پلە</text>
                }
            </p>
        </div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ContributorDto Contributor { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>The bar to the next tier. Off in tight columns where the card has to stay short.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ShowProgress { get; set; } = true;

    TierLevel Level => Tier.For(Contributor.TotalContribution);

    int Pct => (int)Math.Round(Tier.Progress(Contributor.TotalContribution) * 100);
}
